{"maxId":262,"entries":{"chapter-2-study-demography-and-epidemiology|Chapter 2 Study: Demography and Epidemiology":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Improved survival and declining birth rates reshape age distributions; population aging differs across countries and historical periods (p23-25).":["u3"],"copy|Interpret remaining life expectancy at a specified age separately from life expectancy at birth; survival curves reflect changing mortality across the lifespan (p25-26).":["u4"],"copy|Distinguish age effects, period effects shared during an event, and birth-cohort effects from shared historical exposure (p26-27).":["u5"],"copy|The dependency ratio compares conventional working/nonworking age groups; it does not establish an individual's actual work or economic dependence (p27).":["u6"],"copy|US older populations are becoming more diverse. Aggregate survival gains coexist with racial, ethnic and socioeconomic differences in morbidity and disability (p27).":["u7"],"copy|Hypertension is the most common chronic condition among older Medicare beneficiaries; chronic diseases commonly coexist rather than appearing in isolation (p29-31).":["u8"],"copy|In 2018, multiple chronic conditions affected 64% of adults 65 or older, versus 33% at 45-64. Comorbidity relates to an index disease; multimorbidity describes coexisting conditions (p31).":["u9"],"copy|Sensory loss contributes to impaired function and independence; utilization and mortality statistics alone do not describe the full burden (p32-33).":["u10"],"copy|Disability concerns difficulty with daily activities; ADLs capture basic self-care, IADLs more complex independent living. Performance tests add information (p33-34).":["u11"],"copy|Disablement links pathology, impairment, functional limitation and disability; personal and environmental factors modify the pathway. Disability may develop gradually or abruptly and can improve (p36-37).":["u12"],"copy|Cognitive and physical function interact. Screening instruments differ by domain and sensitivity; education and ceiling effects can complicate interpretation (p40).":["u13"],"copy|Biomarkers may reflect disease processes or exposures, but clinical interpretation requires context rather than treating every age association as causation (p40-42).":["u14"],"copy|Physical activity, smoking, alcohol, nutrition and other behaviors influence later-life health; socioeconomic resources shape opportunities to adopt them (p42-43).":["u15"],"copy|Marginalization, discrimination, neighborhood design and social connections influence disability and survival. Address both individual risks and environmental barriers to participation (p43-44).":["u16"],"reading-survival-and-disability-curves|Reading survival and disability curves":["u255"],"population-growth-population-aging-and-remaining-survival|Population growth, population aging and remaining survival":["u256"],"age-historical-period-and-generation|Age, historical period and generation":["u257"],"rates-counts-and-rankings-in-mortality-and-morbidity|Rates, counts and rankings in mortality and morbidity":["u258"],"what-service-and-prescription-data-can-and-cannot-show|What service and prescription data can and cannot show":["u259"],"measuring-function-and-following-transitions|Measuring function and following transitions":["u260"],"cognition-and-biomarkers-in-population-studies|Cognition and biomarkers in population studies":["u261"],"behavior-socioeconomic-resources-and-environment|Behavior, socioeconomic resources and environment":["u262"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Life expectancy at birth and remaining life expectancy after reaching an older age have different denominators (p25–26).":["u26"],"copy|A large number of people with a disease can reflect longer survival after onset as well as new cases (p23–26).":["u27"],"copy|A transition into disability may be followed by recovery. Cross-sectional prevalence does not show the direction of change (p36–37).":["u28"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u29"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u30"],"copy|CHAPTER 2 / HAZZARD":["u18"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 2; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u19"],"copy|Excluded from the IMA required reading list (P005-2026); not cited in the 2020-2026 past papers.":["u20"],"copy|Population aging and its measurement":["u21"],"copy|Disease burden, function and cognition":["u22"],"copy|Modifiable context":["u23"],"copy|Rectangularization describes a change in the shape of the survival curve as more people survive to advanced age.":["u37"],"copy|Compression of morbidity asks when disease or disability begins relative to death.":["u38"],"copy|The chapter describes evidence for late disability among many centenarians, while recognizing continued debate about compression in the population as a whole (p25–26).":["u39"],"copy|A population can age through better survival, fewer births or both.":["u40"],"copy|The US demographic dependency ratio rose from 49 in 2010 to 54 in 2019.":["u41"],"copy|The calculation groups children and older adults together relative to the working-age population.":["u42"],"copy|Its economic interpretation is limited because some older adults work and because assets and transfers also matter (p27–28).":["u43"],"copy|Measure the level at which a difficulty occurs.":["u44"],"copy|Impaired balance or slow gait can precede difficulty walking outdoors, which in turn can prevent independent shopping.":["u45"],"copy|The Short Physical Performance Battery samples standing balance, a 4-meter walk and chair stands.":["u46"],"copy|Functional limitations can emerge abruptly after stroke or accumulate gradually.":["u47"],"copy|Repeated assessment can show recovery that a single disability count would miss (p33–34, p36–37).":["u48"],"copy|Begin with the denominator.":["u49"],"copy|An increase in the number of older people can occur in a growing population without an increase in their proportion.":["u50"],"copy|Population aging means that the age distribution itself has shifted.":["u51"],"copy|Declining fertility narrows the base of the age distribution, while improved survival allows more members of earlier birth cohorts to reach later ages.":["u52"],"copy|Table 2-1 combines observed and projected US counts: the share aged 65 or older rose from 4.1% around the start of the twentieth century to about 12% around the start of the next.":["u53"],"copy|Observed past change and a projection built from assumptions should not be reported as the same kind of evidence (p23–24).":["u54"],"copy|Figure 2-1 compares older population shares across countries and projected dates.":["u55"],"copy|Countries can reach a similar older share by different routes and at different speeds.":["u56"],"copy|Rapid reductions in mortality and fertility can allow a transition within a few generations, leaving less time to develop long-term support and health services.":["u57"],"copy|The absolute number of older adults depends on population size as well as the percentage.":["u58"],"copy|A country with a relatively small older share may therefore need services for more older people than a much smaller country whose percentage is higher (p23–25).":["u59"],"copy|Figure 2-2 illustrates the shape of survival curves.":["u60"],"copy|When many early deaths are prevented, a larger proportion of a birth cohort survives into late life and deaths become more concentrated at older ages.":["u61"],"copy|This rectangularization of survival does not specify when chronic illness or disability begins.":["u62"],"copy|Compression of morbidity would require onset to move closer to death, shortening the period spent ill or disabled.":["u63"],"copy|It is possible to live longer while also living longer with disease, so compare disease-free or disability-free time when answering a question about healthy life expectancy (p25–26).":["u64"],"copy|Life expectancy at a given age is conditional on having reached that age.":["u65"],"copy|In Table 2-2's US 2017 figures, the all-race estimates at birth were 76 years for males and 81 years for females.":["u66"],"copy|At 65, remaining life expectancy was 18 years for males and 21 years for females; at 85, the corresponding values were 5.9 and 7 years.":["u67"],"copy|Subtracting eighty-five from life expectancy at birth would give a nonsensical answer because the older person has already survived risks that contributed to the estimate at birth.":["u68"],"copy|These are population averages that vary with health and other circumstances (p25).":["u69"],"copy|Extreme longevity requires accurate age documentation and an appropriate comparison cohort.":["u70"],"copy|The chapter describes geographic clusters of unusually long-lived people but notes that proposed explanations such as activity, diet and social connection are not validated causal accounts of a particular cluster.":["u71"],"copy|In the New England centenarian study, siblings of centenarians had 8–17 times the likelihood of reaching 100 compared with others from the same birth cohort.":["u72"],"copy|Family aggregation can motivate genetic research but does not exclude shared environment, and determinants of exceptional survival need not explain most variation in ordinary lifespan (p26).":["u73"],"copy|Figure 2-3 separates age effects from period and birth-cohort effects.":["u74"],"copy|An age effect describes a change associated with getting older.":["u75"],"copy|A period effect arises when an event or change in care affects people of many ages at roughly the same calendar time.":["u76"],"copy|A cohort effect follows people born during a particular period who shared an exposure or opportunity.":["u77"],"copy|A single comparison of people aged forty and eighty cannot uniquely assign the difference to biological aging, because they also belong to different cohorts.":["u78"],"copy|Repeated observations across ages, dates and cohorts provide a stronger basis for interpretation (p26–27).":["u79"],"copy|Figures 2-4 and 2-5 show that the composition of the older US population changes as well as its size.":["u80"],"copy|The first plots men per 100 women across older age ranges, while the second shows projected changes in racial and ethnic composition.":["u81"],"copy|A sex ratio is not a survival probability, and the percentage of older people belonging to a demographic group is not that group's risk of disease.":["u82"],"copy|Differential survival, migration, cohort size and socioeconomic opportunity can all affect the population from which disease and disability data are drawn (p27–28).":["u83"],"copy|Counts of people in conventional working-age and dependent-age bands are useful for planning but do not directly measure financial dependence.":["u84"],"copy|Some older people remain employed, provide unpaid care or draw on accumulated resources.":["u85"],"copy|Some people in the conventionally productive age range are not employed.":["u86"],"copy|Retirement patterns vary by sex, cohort and economic conditions, while widowhood and living arrangements affect the availability of support.":["u87"],"copy|Ask what the dependency ratio actually counted before using it to make a claim about who supports whom in a household (p27–28).":["u88"],"copy|Table 2-3 gives several answers to the question “How large is heart disease as a cause of death?”":["u89"],"copy|Among US adults aged 65 or older in 2017, the reported rate was 1,021 deaths per 100,000 population, accounting for 25% of deaths in that age group.":["u90"],"copy|For malignancies the corresponding figures were 841 per 100,000 and 21% of deaths.":["u91"],"copy|The rate uses everyone in the specified population as its denominator; the proportion uses only those who died.":["u92"],"copy|A cause can represent a higher share of deaths because another cause became less common, even when its own rate is unchanged (p29).":["u93"],"copy|Figure 2-6 separates age-specific rates for leading causes rather than assigning the all-older-adult rate to each age band.":["u94"],"copy|Figure 2-7 adds calendar time, sex and age for cancer mortality.":["u95"],"copy|An increase in total deaths can accompany declining age-specific mortality when the number of people surviving to high-risk ages rises.":["u96"],"copy|For comparisons across places or eras with different population structures, state whether the rates are crude, age specific or adjusted.":["u97"],"copy|A ranking alone does not reveal whether the absolute risk in the group changed (p29–30).":["u98"],"copy|For chronic disease, distinguish incidence from prevalence and duration.":["u99"],"copy|New cases add to prevalence, and death or recovery removes cases.":["u100"],"copy|Effective treatment that prolongs survival after diagnosis can raise the number living with disease even if incidence falls.":["u101"],"copy|Conversely, low recorded prevalence can reflect failure to recognize disease, differences in coding or exclusion of institutionalized people.":["u102"],"copy|Table 2-4 describes conditions per 100 Medicare beneficiaries aged 65 or older in the specified year, with hypertension the commonest.":["u103"],"copy|The table describes that covered population, not every older adult worldwide (p29–31).":["u104"],"copy|Multimorbidity counts coexisting chronic conditions without designating one primary illness.":["u105"],"copy|Comorbidity is defined in relation to an index disease.":["u106"],"copy|In the chapter's 2018 comparison, 64% of adults aged 65 or older had multiple chronic conditions compared with 33% of those aged 45–64.":["u107"],"copy|Figure 2-8 follows accumulation over time across racial and ethnic groups.":["u108"],"copy|Observed differences raise questions about lifelong exposures, access and survival; they are not proof that a group identity itself produces a particular trajectory.":["u109"],"copy|The number of diagnoses also does not specify their severity or functional consequences (p31).":["u110"],"copy|Figure 2-9 shows prescription use over a defined recent period by age.":["u111"],"copy|The chapter reports that about two-thirds of adults aged 65 or older used at least three drugs, and 40% used at least five.":["u112"],"copy|Medication count reflects disease burden and care processes and can flag interaction or treatment burden, but does not by itself demonstrate inappropriate prescribing.":["u113"],"copy|It also matters whether a survey counts prescriptions dispensed, medicines reported as taken or all products actually used.":["u114"],"copy|Those measures need not identify the same people (p31–32).":["u115"],"copy|Table 2-5 reports visits, not unique patients.":["u116"],"copy|In the 2016 US data, people aged 75 or older had 547 outpatient visits per 100 people per year, and 54% of visits were for chronic problems.":["u117"],"copy|This does not imply that 547 percent of patients visited or that every patient had the same frequency.":["u118"],"copy|A subgroup with very frequent visits can increase the average while another subgroup faces serious access barriers.":["u119"],"copy|Table 2-6 uses the first-listed diagnosis for hospitalizations, so it does not enumerate every disease contributing to each admission (p32–33).":["u120"],"copy|Figures 2-10 and 2-11 deal with different measures.":["u121"],"copy|The cardiovascular figure plots prevalent heart failure, stroke and coronary disease by age and sex.":["u122"],"copy|The cancer figure shows incidence rates for particular cancers in men and women by age.":["u123"],"copy|High prevalence may reflect many long-term survivors, whereas a high incidence rate refers to new events over time.":["u124"],"copy|Sensory impairments show why neither death nor hospitalization captures the full clinical burden: untreated hearing or visual loss can restrict communication, mobility and participation while never becoming the first-listed reason for hospital admission (p32–35).":["u125"],"copy|Ask precisely what the respondent was asked.":["u126"],"copy|Having difficulty bathing, needing someone to assist with bathing and not attempting to bathe are related but distinct states.":["u127"],"copy|Instrumental tasks such as shopping, managing medicine and handling money make cognitive and environmental demands beyond those of basic self-care.":["u128"],"copy|A person may report no problem shopping because someone else has always done it.":["u129"],"copy|Pair self-report with direct performance when feasible and learn what help or equipment the person already uses (p33–35).":["u130"],"copy|The Short Physical Performance Battery samples standing balance, usual walking across 4 meters and repeated chair rises.":["u131"],"copy|These tasks assess capacity under test conditions rather than actual participation at home.":["u132"],"copy|A person who walks adequately along a corridor may still be unable to shop if streets are unsafe or public transportation inaccessible.":["u133"],"copy|In the opposite direction, an aid or an adapted environment can preserve daily independence despite low measured capacity.":["u134"],"copy|Describe impairment, task limitation and reliance on assistance separately (p33–34, p36–37).":["u135"],"copy|Figure 2-12 compares disability status across Medicare beneficiaries by age, sex and racial or ethnic group and includes institutional care.":["u136"],"copy|Figure 2-13 separates prevalent mobility disability from incidence in people who were initially nondisabled and from subsequent transitions.":["u137"],"copy|These denominators matter.":["u138"],"copy|Higher prevalence can result from more new cases, a lower probability of recovery, or survival with disability for a longer interval.":["u139"],"copy|A cross-sectional survey cannot reveal which of these pathways explains the difference (p34–36).":["u140"],"copy|In one cohort with initial impairment, 2 years later 4% had died, 9% had declined further, 50% remained impaired and 37% had recovered.":["u141"],"copy|Thus a disability count is a snapshot of movement into, through and out of impaired states.":["u142"],"copy|Death is an alternative outcome, not merely a person missing from follow-up.":["u143"],"copy|If only survivors are examined, the apparent probability of recovery will be conditional on surviving, and may differ from the probability the original patient or cohort would want to know (p36–37).":["u144"],"copy|The disablement pathway moves from disease or injury to an impairment, an altered capacity to perform a task and potentially a restriction in everyday activities.":["u145"],"copy|Both the person and the environment modify each step.":["u146"],"copy|A stroke can produce an abrupt change; accumulating disease and reduced reserve can produce gradual loss.":["u147"],"copy|Figure 2-14 follows contrasting patterns of disability near death and shows why the approach to a medically similar endpoint need not look alike.":["u148"],"copy|Planning for a person with longstanding severe dependence differs from planning for someone who had little disability until a final rapid decline (p36–37).":["u149"],"copy|Figure 2-15 relates the number of chronic conditions to predicted limitations across age and sex groups.":["u150"],"copy|It illustrates aggregation of risk rather than a deterministic conversion of one diagnosis into one lost activity.":["u151"],"copy|Figure 2-16 then asks how the number of people needing ADL help changes as cohorts age.":["u152"],"copy|Even a declining individual probability at a given age can leave more people requiring care if the group reaching that age grows.":["u153"],"copy|For services planning, examine both age-specific rates and the projected absolute number needing assistance (p38–39).":["u154"],"copy|A brief cognitive instrument samples selected domains; results depend on the tasks, education, language, sensory function and severity range represented.":["u155"],"copy|A ceiling effect can mask differences among people at a higher level of function.":["u156"],"copy|Clinical dementia involves more than a low screening score, and a diagnosis recorded in an administrative database reflects recognition and coding as well as disease.":["u157"],"copy|A study using face-to-face assessment can therefore yield a different estimate from a claims analysis without either being a simple arithmetic error (p39–40).":["u158"],"copy|Figure 2-17 pools age- and sex-specific dementia prevalence from European cohorts.":["u159"],"copy|The shape of the curve shows the importance of age structure: a sample containing more very old participants can have higher overall prevalence even if each age-specific rate matches another sample.":["u160"],"copy|Survival after diagnosis also matters.":["u161"],"copy|A cross-sectional difference between women and men is not, on its own, evidence of a difference in incidence.":["u162"],"copy|For an individual with fluctuating attention or sensory impairment, a population prevalence does not substitute for assessing the immediate clinical presentation (p39–40).":["u163"],"copy|A biomarker can indicate exposure, a biological response, disease or prognosis.":["u164"],"copy|A marker associated with age is not automatically a cause of aging or a validated surrogate for disability or survival.":["u165"],"copy|Composite measures combine information across several systems; physiological tests, imaging and molecular profiles capture different aspects of reserve or pathology.":["u166"],"copy|An epigenetic clock trained to estimate chronological age can predict what it was trained to predict without proving that a treatment which changes its value prolongs healthy life.":["u167"],"copy|Look for longitudinal prediction of meaningful outcomes and independent validation (p40–42).":["u168"],"copy|Exposure measurement can also determine which explanation looks plausible.":["u169"],"copy|A blood test captures a particular biological state at the time of collection.":["u170"],"copy|A gait test can integrate the consequences of multiple chronic conditions, deconditioning and neurological or sensory impairment.":["u171"],"copy|A neighborhood measure may reflect exposures shared by many residents over years.":["u172"],"copy|If all three predict later disability, they need not be competitors for the title of “the cause.”":["u173"],"copy|They can lie at different stages of the same pathway.":["u174"],"copy|Ask whether the study measures a pre-existing risk factor, early manifestation of disease, functional consequence or protective adaptation, and whether repeated measurements demonstrate the proposed order of events (p36–37, p40–44).":["u175"],"copy|Table 2-7 compares behavioral factors in US middle-aged and older groups in 2018.":["u176"],"copy|In the survey, 13% of those aged 45–64 and 6.3% of those aged 65 or older reported everyday smoking; former smoking was 26% and 40%, respectively.":["u177"],"copy|This is a cross-sectional comparison of survivors.":["u178"],"copy|Differences can reflect quitting, cohort history and differential mortality, not a simple within-person effect of age.":["u179"],"copy|Survey definitions also matter: any alcohol use in the last month is a different measure from a high weekly quantity or drinking heavily on one occasion (p42).":["u180"],"copy|Education, income, neighborhood resources, discrimination and social networks influence exposure, access to prevention, probability of diagnosis and chances of maintaining function after disease begins.":["u181"],"copy|An exercise recommendation is difficult to implement where sidewalks are unsafe or a person has no affordable transport.":["u182"],"copy|Conversely accessible housing and practical help can reduce the disability produced by the same physical impairment.":["u183"],"copy|Do not reduce an observed socioeconomic difference to individual choices when environments and opportunities differ (p42–44).":["u184"],"copy|When comparing decline across racial or ethnic groups, ask which exposures, social conditions, migration histories and health-care barriers are represented by the categories used.":["u185"],"copy|Marginalization and structural disadvantage can operate over the life course, and groups include substantial variation within them.":["u186"],"copy|A population association can identify a need for equitable intervention without predicting an individual's abilities or preferences.":["u187"],"copy|In a clinical assessment, ask what obstacle actually limits participation; in a population study, define the exposure and denominator clearly enough to distinguish that obstacle from differences in age, case finding and survival (p31, p42–44).":["u188"],"copy|No past exam questions are mapped to this chapter in the 2020–2026 reference list.":["u189"],"copy|1.":["u190"],"copy|Which two demographic changes increase the proportion of older adults?":["u191"],"copy|2.":["u192"],"copy|What does rectangularization of the survival curve describe?":["u193"],"copy|3.":["u194"],"copy|What would constitute compression of morbidity?":["u195"],"copy|4.":["u196"],"copy|Why use remaining life expectancy for someone already aged 65?":["u197"],"copy|5.":["u198"],"copy|How does a cohort effect differ from a period effect?":["u199"],"copy|6.":["u200"],"copy|Why is the age-based dependency ratio an imperfect estimate of financial burden?":["u201"],"copy|7.":["u202"],"copy|Which chronic condition was most common among older Medicare beneficiaries in the chapter?":["u203"],"copy|8.":["u204"],"copy|How do comorbidity and multimorbidity differ?":["u205"],"copy|9.":["u206"],"copy|What age contrast in US multiple chronic conditions did the chapter report for 2018?":["u207"],"copy|10.":["u208"],"copy|Why can shopping reveal difficulty before a person needs help dressing?":["u209"],"copy|11.":["u210"],"copy|What three types of performance does the SPPB sample?":["u211"],"copy|12.":["u212"],"copy|How do progressive and catastrophic disability differ?":["u213"],"copy|13.":["u214"],"copy|Why repeat functional assessment after an episode of disability?":["u215"],"copy|14.":["u216"],"copy|Why interpret a near-perfect cognitive screening score cautiously?":["u217"],"copy|15.":["u218"],"copy|How can a neighborhood change disability without changing a diagnosis?":["u219"],"copy|Answer":["u220","u222","u224","u226","u228","u230","u232","u234","u236","u241","u243","u247","u249","u251","u253"],"copy|Declining fertility reduces the relative number of younger people, and improving survival allows more people to reach advanced ages. (p23–25)":["u221"],"copy|More of the population surviving until late life before deaths become concentrated at older ages. (p25)":["u223"],"copy|A later onset of morbidity or disability relative to the end of life, concentrating the period lived with illness or disability nearer death. (p26)":["u225"],"copy|That person has survived risks occurring before that age. A figure calculated at birth includes those earlier deaths. (p25–26)":["u227"],"copy|A cohort shares exposures associated with its birth period. A period effect reflects an event or circumstance experienced across age groups at a particular time. (p26–27)":["u229"],"copy|Many people classified by age as dependent still work, and assets and transfers also affect dependence. (p27–28)":["u231"],"copy|Hypertension. The distribution derives from a particular covered population, and several conditions can coexist in one person. (p29–31)":["u233"],"copy|Comorbidity is considered relative to an index disease. Multimorbidity describes multiple conditions without designating one as primary. (p31)":["u235"],"copy|64%":["u237"],"copy|among adults 65 or older, compared with":["u238"],"copy|33%":["u239"],"copy|among adults 45–64. (p31)":["u240"],"copy|Shopping is an instrumental activity that combines mobility, planning and community access. Dressing is a basic self-care task. (p33)":["u242"],"copy|Standing balance, gait speed over":["u244"],"copy|4 meters":["u245"],"copy|and repeated chair stands as a measure of lower-extremity strength. (p34)":["u246"],"copy|One develops gradually. The other can begin suddenly after an acute event such as a stroke. (p37)":["u248"],"copy|Disability can improve and recur. Measuring only at onset may wrongly imply permanent dependence. (p36–37)":["u250"],"copy|A ceiling effect can obscure subtle differences. Education, the domains assessed and physical or sensory function affect interpretation. (p39–40)":["u252"],"copy|Transport, street design and social resources can facilitate or restrict activity and participation for someone with the same underlying impairment. (p43–44)":["u254"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[264,-237],[265,-210],[266,-2],[267,-115]]}
